From 2ec0d93ed31428f367737b63547e9579cd06c747 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:13:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] listeners: add a GPT partition listener for the UEFI variable
 store

QTEE reaches GPT partitions of the non-secure storage through listener
0x2001, and uefisecapp keeps the UEFI variable store in one. Without the
listener, non-volatile UEFI variable writes from Linux fail with
EFI_DEVICE_ERROR.

The listener serves the variable store and nothing else, and checks it
before registering, so a mistake costs a failed variable write rather
than other partitions:

- It looks only on UFS logical units 4 and 0, where UEFI keeps the
  store, never on removable media, and wants exactly one partition of
  the store's type in a GPT whose header and entry CRCs check out.
- It opens the kernel's block device for that partition, exclusively,
  after matching its number, extent and device number against the GPT
  entry, so the kernel bounds every access to the partition.
- It reads the store's table of contents from its first block and lets
  writes land only inside the tables listed there. A store it can't
  parse gets no listener at all.

UEFI serves the listener until ExitBootServices and uefisecapp keeps
using the context IDs UEFI handed out without sending INIT again. UEFI
opens only the variable store through it, so the listener takes a
context ID it didn't hand out to mean the variable store.

GPTSVC_MODE=ro or log refuse writes, GPTSVC_SHADOW serves the store
from a file, and GPTSVC_DEBUG dumps each sector, for bring-up.
